lucas (android): QA hit weirdness in Kotlin onboarding sample: when token refresh takes >2s coroutine callers sometimes never get a response, and occasionally we see duplicate refresh calls. repro on Android 11+ with Retrofit coroutines adapter.

mike (sdk): duplicate refreshes often mean two concurrent calls observed 401 and both kicked off refresh. Do you use a global synchronized coalescer?

lucas (android): we tried an in-authenticator coalesce but copied pattern from Java sample. our coroutine adapter uses `suspendCoroutine` -> `call.enqueue(...)` and we call `call.clone()` on retry.

lucas (android): stack: UI launches `viewModelScope.launch { api.uploadPhoto(file) }` -> Retrofit coroutine adapter -> OkHttp -> 401 -> Authenticator does blocking refresh via Retrofit `.execute()` on same client -> after refresh we `call.clone().execute()` from Authenticator, sometimes it hangs.

hema (devex): there are a couple of tricky parity bits: 1) calling blocking `.execute()` on same client can deadlock if dispatcher threads are exhausted (especially with coroutines adapter that pins threads). 2) Retrofit coroutine adapters historically wrapped cancellations differently so Deadline/timeout isn't propagated to underlying OkHttp call. 3) call.clone() vs enqueue interplay with coroutine cancellation needs explicit wiring.

mike (sdk): recommended changes: - use a separate OkHttpClient for auth refreshes (small pool) - make Authenticator return null and surface 401 to app logic so you can coalesce in coroutine layer - ensure coroutine adapter registers cancellation handler that calls `call.cancel()` and also `call.timeout().cancel()` if supported.

lucas (android): can you show the cancellation wiring for suspendCancellableCoroutine?

mike (sdk): example we use in SDK samples:

```kotlin
suspend fun <T> Call.awaitResponse(): Response<T> = suspendCancellableCoroutine { cont ->
  enqueue(object : Callback<T> {
    override fun onResponse(call: Call<T>, res: Response<T>) { cont.resume(res) }
    override fun onFailure(call: Call<T>, t: Throwable) { cont.resumeWithException(t) }
  })

  cont.invokeOnCancellation {
    try {
      // best-effort: cancel OkHttp call + cancel call timeout to interrupt blocking writes
      call.cancel()
      call.timeout().cancel()
    } catch (e: Exception) { /* ignore */ }
  }
}
```

mike (sdk): also coalesce refreshes with a singleFlight: use `Mutex` + cached Deferred token; if a coroutine sees token is refreshing await the Deferred rather than starting new refresh. Avoid doing the refresh inside Authenticator.

tara (qa): QA observed the hang correlates with large multipart uploads (streaming) + coroutine cancellation. If upload is canceled during refresh, the coroutine never resumes and resource leaks.

mike (sdk): addendum: streaming bodies + coroutine adapter + cancellation require two things: 1) make RequestBody cancel-aware (check `Thread.currentThread().isInterrupted()` or AtomicBoolean) 2) adapter cancellation must close streaming source (e.g., keep reference to `Source` and close it in invokeOnCancellation). We'll add a sample showing `CancellableStreamingRequestBody`.

lucas (android): action: I'll patch sample to use app-level coalescer (return null from Authenticator) + demo suspendCancellableCoroutine snippet and a `CancellableStreamingRequestBody`. Also add note about `call.timeout().cancel()` for aggressive interruption.

hema (devex): excellent. please add an onboarding doc entry "Kotlin coroutine adapter: cancellation, timeouts and auth coalescing" and include small test we can run on emulator/device matrix.

